-- The barrier's half of the layout guard. Sibling of
-- every-join-misindented.l4, which pins the same defect for the fork.
--
-- BOTH join spellings are bodiless -- `ONCE ALL HAVE` and `UPON EACH` each
-- carry no expression of their own -- so neither has a body whose indentation
-- would catch a misplaced line. That is why every WORD of a join line is
-- column-checked (L4.Parser.joinLine), and why both spellings need a fixture:
-- the guard is per-alternative, and a refactor could easily restore one and
-- lose the other.
--
-- Here the author writes `ONCE ALL HAVE` at the OUTER rule's clause column,
-- meaning it for the outer EVERY -- but a nested EVERY is still open inside the
-- outer's HENCE, and the nested one's clauses live further right. Without the
-- guard this parsed clean, attached the BARRIER to the inner rule, and
-- exactprinted byte-for-byte, so nothing showed the author that the join had
-- bound to the wrong rule.
--
-- As in the fork's fixture, a misplaced HEAD keyword is reported by the
-- expression follow-set rather than as an indentation error; only a misplaced
-- second word (ALL, HAVE, EACH) says "incorrect indentation".

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Tenant HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign HAS signer IS AN Actor

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`bad` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant x
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY x)
        WITHIN 1
        UPON   EACH
        HENCE  EVERY Tenant y
                   MUST   Sign (EXACTLY y)
                   WITHIN 2
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
